 Ingress vs LoadBalancer – Simplifying Kubernetes Networking
When exposing applications in Kubernetes, two common approaches are LoadBalancer and Ingress. While both serve the purpose of routing external traffic into the cluster, their scope and efficiency differ:
• LoadBalancer
• Directly provisions a cloud load balancer for a single service.
• Each application requires its own load balancer.
• Simple, but not cost‑effective at scale.
• Formula: 1 LoadBalancer = 1 Service = 1 App
• Ingress
• Acts as a smart entry point with routing rules.
• A single load balancer can handle multiple services and apps.
• Supports path‑based and host‑based routing (e.g., , , ).
• More scalable, flexible, and cost‑efficient.
• Formula: 1 LoadBalancer = Multiple Services = Multiple Apps

👉 Key takeaway:
If you’re running just one app, a LoadBalancer works fine. But as soon as you scale to multiple apps or microservices, Ingress becomes the strategic choice—reducing overhead, saving costs, and enabling smarter traffic management.
